<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1052">1052</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad was the Uqaylid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and the most important ruler of the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a>, reigning from 1001 to about 1050. He held northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, briefly recognising the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 1010 before returning to Abbasid allegiance. He was a patron of poets, and in his last years he was deposed by his brother Baraka and died in captivity in 1052.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qirwash-ibn-al-muqallad.jpg" alt="Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27tamid_al-Dawla_Qirwash_1001-1050_AR_Dirham_Awana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Qirwash was the eldest son of al-Muqallad ibn al-Musayyab, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> from 996, who had taken Anbar, Tikrit and other towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> almost to the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> before he was assassinated in 1001 while plotting to seize the city from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>. Uqaylid power rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribesmen rather than a salaried standing army, and his death threw the tribal confederation into turmoil. Qirwash and his uncle al-Hasan both claimed the leadership, then made common cause against a third claimant, Qarrad ibn Ladid, sharing the revenue of Mosul while Qirwash took over his father's rule at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-asad">Banu Asad</a>, Qirwash defeated a Buyid army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> on 22 July 1002, but the Buyid commander al-Hajjaj ibn Ustadh Hurmuz, helped by the Khafaja and the Kurdish Annazids, then beat the allies at Baziqiya. In 1005/6 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> gave Kufa back to him, and in Baghdad Caliph al-Qadir granted him the title Muʿtamid al-Dawla. His turn to the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim bi-Amr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, whose name was read in the Friday sermon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> on 18 August 1010, collapsed when the Buyids moved against him, and it prompted al-Qadir's anti-Fatimid Baghdad Manifesto.</p><p>The Buyid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Dawla took Qirwash's sister or daughter Jibara as his wife, and with Uqaylid backing he secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and the marshlands. In 1020 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-asad">Banu Asad</a> and Buyid troops defeated and captured Qirwash, though he escaped, and a Khafaja invasion in 1026/7 ended Uqaylid rule south of Baghdad, although he kept Anbar. In the 1030s he supported the Buyid emir Jalal al-Dawla and in 1039 had the former vizier Ibn Makula strangled. On 21 April 1044, allied with Dubays, he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> at Ra's al-Ayil and drove them back to Adharbayjan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1048/9 his brother Abu Kamil Baraka had become a rival. In June 1049 the brothers' armies met, several of Qirwash's followers went over to Baraka, and Qirwash was captured and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. He remained nominal emir for a while, but real power passed to Baraka, who imprisoned him in 1050. Baraka died in 1052 and was succeeded by Badran's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>. Shortly afterwards, on 27 October 1052, Qirwash died at the fortress of al-Jarrahiya; according to at least one report he was killed on his nephew's orders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad</b>, who reigned for about half a century from 1001, briefly recognised the Fatimid Caliphate in 1010 before returning to the Abbasids.</blockquote><a href="/state/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=1042" aria-label="Mosul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5635 853 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6085 1123h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6085 1123h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=1042">What was here in 1042? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
